# CI workflow security (GitHub Actions & GitLab CI)

Narrows `core-common-rules`' security rules to workflow-YAML risks, following GitHub's Actions security hardening guide. Applies only to GitHub Actions workflows under `.github/workflows/`, composite `action.yml`/`action.yaml` under `.github/actions/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml` and GitLab CI included config under `.gitlab/`.

## `pull_request_target` and untrusted fork code

- `pull_request_target` runs in the **base** repository's context: every repo
  secret the workflow references is available and `GITHUB_TOKEN` gets read/write
  permissions by default (unless a `permissions:` block narrows it), even when the
  pull request comes from an untrusted fork.
- Never check out the pull request's head ref/SHA
  (`actions/checkout` with `ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}`) inside
  a `pull_request_target` job and then run a build/test/lint command against it
  (the "pwn request" pattern).
- If a `pull_request_target` job must read fork content (e.g. to comment on a PR),
  check it out read-only and execute nothing from it (no `run:`, build or test
  step against the fork's code), or split through a privilege-separated
  `workflow_run` (a build job with no secrets, a separate privileged job that
  consumes only safe artifacts).
- When a job only builds/tests a contribution and needs no base-repo secrets or
  write access, use `pull_request`: from a fork, `GITHUB_TOKEN` is read-only and
  no other repo secret is passed.

## Pin third-party actions to a commit SHA

- Reference a third-party action by its full-length commit SHA
  (`uses: actions/checkout@<40-char-sha>`), not a mutable tag (`@v4`) or branch.
- The SHA-pinning rule applies to every action outside the repository's own
  `.github/actions/`, including `actions/*`; no well-known org is exempt.
- A trailing tag comment (`# v4.2.0`) is documentation only; the SHA is the pin.

## Least-privilege `permissions:`

- Default to `permissions: {}` (or `permissions: read-all` only when read access
  is needed everywhere) at the workflow level, then grant specific scopes
  (`contents: write`, `pull-requests: write`, `id-token: write` for OIDC) at the
  **job** level. No workflow-level `permissions: write-all`, and no missing
  `permissions:` key, which can default to broad access per repository/org setting.
- Grant `id-token: write` only to the job that performs OIDC federation.

## Script injection via untrusted expression expansion

- Never interpolate an untrusted `${{ github.event.* }}` value (PR title, issue
  body, commit message, branch name) directly into a `run:` string.
- Pass it through an intermediate `env:` variable and reference it quoted
  (`"$TITLE"`) inside the script.
- GitLab CI's mechanism differs and the `env:` indirection does not carry over:
  every GitLab CI/CD variable (predefined like `CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE`, or under
  `variables:`) is already a shell environment variable when `script:` runs;
  wrapping it in another `variables:` entry changes nothing. The real risks:
  - **Unquoted expansion**: `script: - rm -rf $CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE` word-splits
    and globs an untrusted value; always quote (`"$CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE"`), and
    never concatenate an untrusted variable into a string for `eval`/`sh -c`.
  - **Config-time interpolation via CI/CD inputs** (`$[[ inputs.x ]]` in a
    component/included config's `rules:`/`script:`) is substituted into the YAML
    before the job is created, the same class as `${{ }}`: do not feed an untrusted
    pipeline or trigger input (a `spec:inputs` value, not a predefined variable
    like an MR title) into a `$[[ ]]` interpolation that reaches a `script:` line
    or a `rules:if:` expression without validating/escaping it.

## GitLab CI protected variables and branches

- A "Protected" CI/CD variable is exposed only to pipelines on a protected branch
  or tag; on an unprotected ref (including a fork merge request) it is empty, not
  an error. Do not rely on it in a job whose `rules:`/`only:`/`except:` allow a
  non-protected ref.
- Mark any variable carrying a credential, deploy token or signing secret both
  "Protected" and "Masked", and restrict the consuming job to protected
  branches/tags via `rules:`.
